# FATIGUE AND RECOVERY

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The Fatigue and Recovery model describes how training stress accumulates, how recovery occurs, and how both influence future performance.

Fatigue is not the opposite of fitness.

Fatigue is a temporary consequence of training.

Recovery is the biological process that transforms fatigue into adaptation.

Understanding both is essential for intelligent programming.

---

# Philosophy

Training creates fatigue.

Recovery transforms fatigue into adaptation.

Adaptation increases performance.

Without fatigue there is little adaptation.

Without recovery there is no adaptation.

CAS seeks the optimal balance.

---

# The Adaptation Cycle

Training

↓

Fatigue

↓

Recovery

↓

Adaptation

↓

Higher Capacity

↓

New Training Stimulus

This cycle repeats continuously throughout an athlete's career.

---

# Definition of Fatigue

Fatigue is a temporary reduction in the ability to produce physical or mental performance.

Fatigue is multidimensional.

It cannot be represented by a single number.

---

# Dimensions of Fatigue

CAS distinguishes:

Neurological Fatigue

Muscular Fatigue

Cardiovascular Fatigue

Metabolic Fatigue

Psychological Fatigue

Structural Fatigue

Technical Fatigue

Each dimension follows different recovery timelines.

---

# Neurological Fatigue

Characteristics

Reduced motor unit recruitment.

Reduced rate of force development.

Slower reaction time.

Reduced coordination.

Typical Causes

Heavy strength training.

Explosive work.

Maximal efforts.

Olympic lifting.

Heavy sparring.

Typical Recovery

24–72 hours.

---

# Muscular Fatigue

Characteristics

Reduced force production.

Muscle soreness.

Temporary weakness.

Typical Causes

High volume.

Mechanical tension.

Eccentric loading.

Typical Recovery

24–96 hours.

---

# Cardiovascular Fatigue

Characteristics

Elevated heart rate.

Reduced aerobic efficiency.

Slower recovery between efforts.

Typical Causes

Long conditioning sessions.

High-intensity intervals.

Repeated rounds.

Typical Recovery

Several hours to 48 hours.

---

# Metabolic Fatigue

Characteristics

Reduced energy availability.

Accumulation of metabolic by-products.

Reduced work capacity.

Typical Causes

Repeated high-intensity efforts.

Combat conditioning.

Circuit training.

Typical Recovery

Hours to one day.

---

# Psychological Fatigue

Characteristics

Reduced motivation.

Reduced concentration.

Decision fatigue.

Emotional exhaustion.

Typical Causes

Competition.

Life stress.

Sleep deprivation.

Training monotony.

Psychological fatigue strongly influences training quality.

---

# Structural Fatigue

Characteristics

Stress on tendons.

Ligaments.

Joints.

Connective tissue.

Recovery is considerably slower than muscular recovery.

---

# Technical Fatigue

Characteristics

Reduced movement quality.

Poor timing.

Reduced precision.

Technical fatigue often appears before complete physical fatigue.

---

# Acute Fatigue

Temporary.

Expected.

Necessary for adaptation.

Usually resolves with adequate recovery.

---

# Accumulated Fatigue

Develops across multiple sessions.

May improve adaptation when managed correctly.

Requires intelligent planning.

---

# Functional Overreaching

Temporary reduction in performance.

Followed by improved performance after recovery.

May be intentionally programmed.

---

# Non-Functional Overreaching

Performance declines.

Recovery becomes prolonged.

Adaptation decreases.

Programming should change.

---

# Overtraining Syndrome

Persistent maladaptation.

Reduced performance.

Persistent fatigue.

Psychological symptoms.

Requires medical evaluation.

CAS should never attempt diagnosis.

---

# Recovery

Recovery restores the athlete's capacity to adapt.

Recovery is active.

Not passive.

---

# Recovery Systems

Sleep

Nutrition

Hydration

Active Recovery

Stress Management

Mobility

Time

No single intervention replaces the others.

---

# Sleep

Sleep is the primary recovery mechanism.

It influences:

Neural recovery.

Hormonal regulation.

Motor learning.

Immune function.

Psychological recovery.

Sleep quality is more important than sleep duration alone.

---

# Nutrition

Supports:

Glycogen restoration.

Protein synthesis.

Hydration.

Immune function.

Adaptation.

Nutrition modifies recovery.

It cannot compensate for inadequate sleep.

---

# Active Recovery

Examples

Walking.

Mobility.

Cycling.

Swimming.

Light technical practice.

The objective is recovery,

not additional fatigue.

---

# Recovery Capacity

Recovery capacity differs between athletes.

Influencing factors include:

Age.

Training history.

Sleep.

Nutrition.

Stress.

Workload.

Genetics.

Competition schedule.

CAS individualizes recovery expectations.

---

# Monitoring Recovery

Possible indicators include:

Sleep quality.

Heart-rate variability.

Resting heart rate.

Body mass.

Subjective fatigue.

Muscle soreness.

Performance.

Mood.

Pain.

No single indicator is sufficient.

---

# Fatigue vs Readiness

High fatigue does not always imply poor readiness.

Low fatigue does not always imply high readiness.

The relationship is probabilistic.

CAS evaluates multiple signals simultaneously.

---

# Recovery Debt

Recovery debt accumulates when recovery repeatedly fails to match training stress.

Consequences include:

Reduced adaptation.

Increased injury risk.

Reduced motivation.

Reduced performance.

Programming should prevent chronic recovery debt.

---

# Combat Sports Considerations

Combat athletes accumulate fatigue from:

Strength training.

Conditioning.

Technical practice.

Sparring.

Competition.

Travel.

Weight management.

Recovery must consider all training stress,

not only physical preparation.

---

# Explainability

Example

Today's explosive work has been replaced with technical practice.

Reason

Neurological fatigue remains elevated after yesterday's maximal strength session.

Expected Benefit

Maintain technical progress while allowing neural recovery.

